Northstar Technologies — Synthetic Enterprise Environment
Engineering & DevOps Organizational Knowledge Base
KB Version: 1.0
Classification: Internal
Owner: Platform Engineering
Last Reviewed: 2026-08-15
________________________________________
1. Organization
Northstar Technologies operates a cloud-native SaaS platform primarily hosted on AWS.
The engineering organization is divided into:
Platform Engineering
Application Engineering
Security Engineering
Data Engineering
SRE
Developer Experience
Platform Engineering owns shared infrastructure and cloud standards.
SRE owns reliability, observability and incident response.
Application teams own application code and application-specific deployment configuration.
________________________________________
2. Engineering Environments
Northstar uses three primary environments:
development
staging
production
AWS accounts are separated by environment.
Development
Used for:
•	Feature development
•	Infrastructure experimentation
•	Integration testing
Production data must never be copied into development.
Staging
Used for:
•	Release validation
•	Performance testing
•	Pre-production integration
Staging should closely resemble production architecture.
Production
Used for customer-facing workloads.
Production changes require approved change management.
________________________________________
3. AWS Account Standards
Naming convention:
northstar-dev
northstar-staging
northstar-prod
Resources must include environment metadata.
Required tags:
Environment
Owner
Application
CostCenter
ManagedBy
DataClassification
Example:
Environment=production
Owner=platform-engineering
Application=payments-api
CostCenter=CC-104
ManagedBy=terraform
DataClassification=internal
________________________________________
4. Infrastructure as Code
Terraform is the default infrastructure provisioning tool.
Production infrastructure must not be created manually through the AWS Console unless an emergency procedure explicitly permits it.
Terraform repositories follow:
main
develop
feature/*
Production deployments occur only through the approved CI/CD pipeline.
Terraform state is remotely stored and must never be committed to Git.
________________________________________
5. Terraform Ownership
Platform Engineering owns:
AWS networking
IAM baseline
EKS clusters
Shared observability
Terraform modules
Security controls
Application Engineering owns:
Application-specific infrastructure
Application deployment configuration
Service-level resources
________________________________________
6. Kubernetes Standards
Production workloads run on Amazon EKS.
Namespaces follow:
<team>-<application>
Examples:
payments-api
identity-api
customer-platform
Production workloads must define:
CPU requests
Memory requests
CPU limits
Memory limits
Readiness probes
Liveness probes where appropriate
Production deployments must use approved container registries.
________________________________________
7. Deployment Standard
Production deployments are performed through Git-based CI/CD.
Preferred flow:
Pull Request
    ↓
Code Review
    ↓
Automated Tests
    ↓
Security Scan
    ↓
Build Image
    ↓
Deploy to Staging
    ↓
Validation
    ↓
Production Approval
    ↓
Production Deployment
Direct production deployment from a developer workstation is prohibited.
________________________________________
8. Observability
Production applications must provide:
Application logs
Infrastructure metrics
Application metrics where applicable
Distributed traces for supported services
Health checks
Deployment metadata
Central monitoring is used for:
CPU
Memory
Error rate
Latency
Availability
Container health
AWS service health
________________________________________
9. Incident Severity
SEV-1
Critical customer-facing outage or security incident.
Response:
Immediate escalation
Incident commander assigned
Executive notification where required
Continuous investigation
SEV-2
Major degradation affecting a significant portion of users.
Response:
On-call engineer
Platform/SRE involvement
Active investigation
SEV-3
Limited impact or non-critical operational issue.
Response:
Normal engineering workflow
Ticket required
________________________________________
10. Production Access
Production access follows least privilege.
Junior engineers normally have:
Read-only production access
Log access
Monitoring access
Limited diagnostic permissions
Senior engineers may receive:
Expanded diagnostic permissions
Approved operational permissions
Architects and platform leads may have:
Architecture-level access
Emergency privileges
Privileged operations require appropriate authorization.
________________________________________
11. AI Agent Permissions
The AI DevOps Teammate follows the user's organizational role.
Junior Engineer
Allowed:
Read infrastructure state
Read logs
Read metrics
Read Kubernetes state
Read Terraform plans
Query documentation
Not automatically allowed:
Production deletion
IAM modification
Security-group modification
Terraform apply
Kubernetes destructive operations
Senior Engineer
Allowed:
Advanced diagnostics
Approved remediation workflows
Production investigation
Destructive operations still require policy approval.
Architect
Allowed:
Architecture analysis
Cross-system investigation
Infrastructure design recommendations
Policy analysis
The architect role does not automatically bypass security controls.
________________________________________
12. Secrets
Secrets must be stored using approved secret-management systems.
The AI agent must never return:
Passwords
API keys
AWS secret keys
Private keys
Session tokens
Database credentials
The agent may confirm:
Secret exists
Secret is missing
Secret rotation is required
Secret access failed
without exposing its value.
________________________________________
13. Change Management
Production changes must contain:
Change description
Reason
Risk
Affected resources
Rollback plan
Validation plan
Approver
Emergency changes require post-incident documentation.
________________________________________
14. AI Incident Investigation Policy
The AI agent must follow:
Observe
    ↓
Collect evidence
    ↓
Correlate
    ↓
Compare with organizational standard
    ↓
Determine probable cause
    ↓
Recommend remediation
    ↓
Request approval
    ↓
Execute only if authorized
    ↓
Verify result
    ↓
Record audit event
The agent must never skip directly from:
User request → production modification
________________________________________
15. Source-of-Truth Hierarchy
For organization-specific questions:
1. Live infrastructure evidence
2. Approved organizational documentation
3. Approved architecture records
4. Approved runbooks
5. Vendor documentation
6. General model knowledge
If the sources conflict, the agent must explain the conflict.
________________________________________
16. Unknown Information
If the knowledge base does not contain the required organization-specific information, the agent must say:
"I don't have sufficient organizational context to answer this reliably."
It must NOT invent:
team ownership
AWS account IDs
production architecture
security policies
incident procedures
approval requirements
resource names
________________________________________
17. Knowledge Base Metadata
Every document should contain:
document_id:
title:
owner:
team:
classification:
environment:
application:
version:
effective_date:
review_date:
source:
authority:
Example:
document_id: NS-SEC-004
title: Production Access Standard
owner: Security Engineering
team: Security
classification: Internal
environment: production
version: 2.1
effective_date: 2026-07-01
review_date: 2026-10-01
authority: Policy
________________________________________
18. Knowledge Lifecycle
Documents follow:
Draft
 ↓
Review
 ↓
Approved
 ↓
Published
 ↓
Indexed
 ↓
Consumed by Agent
 ↓
Periodic Review
 ↓
Updated / Archived
Expired or superseded documents must not be treated as authoritative.
________________________________________
19. Agent Grounding Requirement
When an answer depends on organizational policy, the agent should provide the source:
Source:
NS-SEC-004 — Production Access Standard

Relevant policy:
Junior engineers have read-only production access.
The agent should distinguish between:
ORGANIZATIONAL FACT
LIVE INFRASTRUCTURE FACT
MODEL INFERENCE
RECOMMENDATION
________________________________________
20. Golden Rule
The AI DevOps Teammate should behave like a cautious senior engineer:
Do not guess.
Do not fabricate organizational context.
Do not expose secrets.
Do not execute destructive operations without authorization.
Do not confuse documentation with live infrastructure.
Do not confuse recommendations with facts.
Always show evidence.
Always identify uncertainty.


